1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,480
 Hey everyone, Ryan here and welcome back to our orthodontic series.

2
00:00:07,480 --> 00:00:13,760
 So you may have noticed that we have a diagnosis and treatment planning video in most of our

3
00:00:13,760 --> 00:00:17,640
 series for the different topics of the clinical board exam.

4
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:22,640
 And that's on purpose because it's really a staple in the health professions.

5
00:00:22,640 --> 00:00:27,840
 We figure out what the problem is, we plan out how to fix it, and then we do our best

6
00:00:27,840 --> 00:00:29,080
 to fix it.

7
00:00:29,080 --> 00:00:33,060
 And that's what diagnosis and treatment planning is all about.

8
00:00:33,060 --> 00:00:36,960
 In orthodontics though, it looks a little bit different.

9
00:00:36,960 --> 00:00:42,720
 So the full name of this specialty is orthodontics and dentofacial orthopedics.

10
00:00:42,720 --> 00:00:48,380
 So it's not only about straightening teeth, but also correcting malformations of the face.

11
00:00:48,380 --> 00:00:55,760
 So it really boils down to bare basics, broad spectrum, both the teeth and the face.

12
00:00:55,760 --> 00:01:01,200
 Dr. Profit, Dr. Ackerman, and Dr. Sarver are known for redefining what orthodontics

13
00:01:01,200 --> 00:01:06,380
 was all about in the context of the soft tissue paradigm.

14
00:01:06,380 --> 00:01:12,560
 So the objectives of orthodontic treatment under this paradigm can be viewed as macroesthetics,

15
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:19,020
 a beautiful face, miniesthetics, a beautiful smile, and microesthetics zooming all the

16
00:01:19,020 --> 00:01:22,320
 way in for beautiful teeth.

17
00:01:22,320 --> 00:01:28,440
 Now, Dr. Ackerman and Dr. Profit went even further to represent five major characteristics

18
00:01:28,440 --> 00:01:32,120
 of an orthodontic patient via a Venn diagram.

19
00:01:32,120 --> 00:01:35,560
 So let's unpack what this is talking about.

20
00:01:35,560 --> 00:01:41,680
 When diagnosing a patient, we can place our findings into these five categories, facial

21
00:01:41,680 --> 00:01:49,240
 proportions and aesthetics, alignment and symmetry, transverse, anterior, which I'll

22
00:01:49,240 --> 00:01:53,440
 be referring to as AP for short and vertical.

23
00:01:53,440 --> 00:01:59,800
 Now I would normally go through each one of these in more detail one at a time, but it

24
00:01:59,800 --> 00:02:04,800
 was challenging putting this material together because there's so much overlap among these

25
00:02:04,800 --> 00:02:10,080
 five categories as you can see in the Venn diagram.

26
00:02:10,080 --> 00:02:15,160
 They are not clean cut categories, so instead keep these in the back of your mind and as

27
00:02:15,160 --> 00:02:21,280
 we go through the steps of an orthodontic exam, we'll fit things among one or more of

28
00:02:21,280 --> 00:02:23,680
 these categories.

29
00:02:23,680 --> 00:02:28,920
 So remember we said before that orthodontics is all about the teeth and the face, and so

30
00:02:28,920 --> 00:02:36,080
 if we really boil it down to the bare basics, our exam is focused on the teeth as part of

31
00:02:36,080 --> 00:02:40,280
 an intraoral exam and the face as part of an extra oral exam.

32
00:02:40,280 --> 00:02:47,080
 Then we also get some additional information from radiographs, particularly the cephalometric.

33
00:02:47,080 --> 00:02:51,600
 So let's go through each one of these one at a time.

34
00:02:51,600 --> 00:02:54,840
 So let's start with the teeth.

35
00:02:54,840 --> 00:03:00,880
 So malocclusion is a very broad term, it refers to a bad bite.

36
00:03:00,880 --> 00:03:06,080
 Malocclusion is not a disease, but a variation from what is considered ideal.

37
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:12,080
 Now one of the most common factors of a malocclusion is some element of crowding.

38
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:18,160
 The teeth being bunched up together like people in a crowd instead of straight along a nice

39
00:03:18,160 --> 00:03:19,160
 arc.

40
00:03:19,160 --> 00:03:24,840
 15% of adolescents and adults have severe crowding, much more than that are going to have some

41
00:03:24,840 --> 00:03:32,720
 degree of mild to moderate crowding, and then some percentage of the population has spacing.

42
00:03:32,720 --> 00:03:39,440
 And we talked in the last video about crowding being a math problem between two size and

43
00:03:39,440 --> 00:03:41,120
 arch size.

44
00:03:41,120 --> 00:03:48,080
 And we can think of it in the context of etiology that crowding carries a genetic predisposition.

45
00:03:48,080 --> 00:03:55,480
 There's some genetic predisposition to a discrepancy between two size and arch size.

46
00:03:55,480 --> 00:04:03,840
 For etiology specifics, malocclusion has an unknown cause in about 60% of cases.

47
00:04:03,840 --> 00:04:10,160
 The known cause is only in 5% of cases, and then that leaves 30% to 35% of the population

48
00:04:10,160 --> 00:04:13,320
 with a normal occlusion.

49
00:04:13,320 --> 00:04:20,960
 So if we move to the Edward-Angle molar classification, we have four main categories here.

50
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:29,480
 So the first is normal class 1 occlusion, that's at 30% to 35% we saw in the last slide.

51
00:04:29,480 --> 00:04:39,280
 A normal class 1 occlusion in the angle classification means that the mesial buccal cusp of the maxillary

52
00:04:39,280 --> 00:04:47,320
 first molar occludes with the mesial buccal groove of the mandibular first molar, and

53
00:04:47,320 --> 00:04:55,080
 the teeth are aligned along this line of occlusion that goes for both the maxillary and the mandibular

54
00:04:55,080 --> 00:04:57,520
 arches.

55
00:04:57,520 --> 00:05:03,760
 For a class 1 maloclusion, which is the most common one up here, we have that same angle

56
00:05:03,760 --> 00:05:10,520
 class 1 molar relationship where that mesial buccal cusp occludes with the buccal groove

57
00:05:10,520 --> 00:05:17,560
 of the lower molar, but this time the teeth don't align along that line of occlusion

58
00:05:17,560 --> 00:05:25,560
 and there's some amount of crowding, or perhaps spacing, some misalignment, rotated teeth,

59
00:05:25,560 --> 00:05:27,400
 etc.

60
00:05:27,400 --> 00:05:34,520
 For a class 2 maloclusion, that means that the upper molar is too far forward and/or

61
00:05:34,520 --> 00:05:37,480
 the lower molar is too far back.

62
00:05:37,480 --> 00:05:44,480
 Another way to think about it is that the maxillary arch is displaced measly or anteriorly.

63
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:51,920
 The teeth may or may not be incorrectly aligned, even if they were perfectly straight and this

64
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:57,520
 was the molar relationship, we would call it a class 2 occlusion.

65
00:05:57,520 --> 00:06:04,240
 Now we have subdivision 1 and subdivision 2 for the class 2 maloclusions.

66
00:06:04,240 --> 00:06:10,560
 And 1 means that the upper incisors are flared forward or proclined.

67
00:06:10,560 --> 00:06:17,640
 So these front teeth are either flared like this, they're proclined out, and we usually

68
00:06:17,640 --> 00:06:24,560
 have some element of excess over a jet, we'll talk more about that in the next slide.

69
00:06:24,560 --> 00:06:30,600
 Subdivision 2 is where the upper incisors are more upright, more like they're pictured

70
00:06:30,600 --> 00:06:32,720
 in this image.

71
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:38,000
 They're standing up a little bit straighter, they're retroclined, they're upright.

72
00:06:38,000 --> 00:06:42,840
 So in this case you may or may not have this excess over jet in the front, but you will

73
00:06:42,840 --> 00:06:48,240
 have some element of excess over bite.

74
00:06:48,240 --> 00:06:56,000
 In class 3 maloclusion, the upper molar is too far back and/or the lower molar is too

75
00:06:56,000 --> 00:06:58,040
 far forward.

76
00:06:58,040 --> 00:07:04,060
 Another way to think about this is that the maxillary arch is displaced posteriorly, again

77
00:07:04,060 --> 00:07:09,560
 like class 2, the teeth may or may not be incorrectly aligned.

78
00:07:09,560 --> 00:07:15,520
 This is the rarest form of maloclusion of these categories.

79
00:07:15,520 --> 00:07:20,680
 So now we can talk about incisor overlap, and I know I've thrown out these terms over jet

80
00:07:20,680 --> 00:07:27,280
 and over bite throughout the series, now we finally get to unpack what exactly these mean.

81
00:07:27,280 --> 00:07:37,400
 So over jet is defined as the horizontal overlap of incisors measured from the labial surface

82
00:07:37,400 --> 00:07:43,480
 of the lower incisor to the labial surface of the upper incisor.

83
00:07:43,480 --> 00:07:48,760
 So this image actually doesn't portray this accurately, the arrow should actually be going

84
00:07:48,760 --> 00:07:51,000
 all the way up here.

85
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:58,760
 So normally the incisors, or I should say the upper incisors overlap the lower incisors

86
00:07:58,760 --> 00:08:03,240
 just by the thickness of their incisal edge.

87
00:08:03,240 --> 00:08:08,560
 So if the upper incisor is coupled with or butting up right against this lower incisor

88
00:08:08,560 --> 00:08:15,800
 as it should, they're contacting each other, the over jet should be about 2 to 3 millimeters,

89
00:08:15,800 --> 00:08:22,640
 which is just this amount, this thickness of the upper incisal edge.

90
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:30,160
 So this image is of course depicting an excess over jet, class 2 incisors, particularly what

91
00:08:30,160 --> 00:08:34,800
 we would expect in a subdivision 1 malocclusion.

92
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:40,160
 The opposite of that is called reverse over jet, where the lower incisors are actually

93
00:08:40,160 --> 00:08:42,760
 out in front of the upper incisors.

94
00:08:42,760 --> 00:08:51,080
 That would be a class 3 incisor relationship, excess is much more prevalent than reverse.

95
00:08:51,080 --> 00:08:59,800
 For overbite, that means the vertical overlap of the incisors, this is measured from incisal

96
00:08:59,800 --> 00:09:04,480
 edge to incisal edge.

97
00:09:04,480 --> 00:09:12,520
 So normally the lower incisors should again contact the lingual surface of the upper incisors

98
00:09:12,520 --> 00:09:19,440
 but it should be at or above the singular, so somewhere in this region.

99
00:09:19,440 --> 00:09:24,600
 This would create an overbite of about 1 to 2 millimeters.

100
00:09:24,600 --> 00:09:31,160
 So this image is showing somewhat of a deep bite, which means we have excess overbite.

101
00:09:31,160 --> 00:09:37,680
 The opposite of this is if we have some amount of vertical space between the upper and lower

102
00:09:37,680 --> 00:09:41,720
 incisal edges, which is called open bite.

103
00:09:41,720 --> 00:09:47,920
 Deep bite is much more prevalent than open bite.

104
00:09:47,920 --> 00:09:49,920
 Next we have cross bites.

105
00:09:49,920 --> 00:09:56,880
 So the first is anterior cross bite, and this refers to the maxillary anterior teeth being

106
00:09:56,880 --> 00:10:01,040
 lingual to the mandibular anterior teeth.

107
00:10:01,040 --> 00:10:06,680
 So actually another way to refer to this is reverse over jet, just like we talked about

108
00:10:06,680 --> 00:10:15,200
 in the last slide, where those lower incisors are out in front of the upper incisors.

109
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:18,620
 Poster cross bite is a little bit more complex.

110
00:10:18,620 --> 00:10:27,280
 This refers to some mismatch in horizontal overlap of the back teeth.

111
00:10:27,280 --> 00:10:36,280
 So we know that the upper teeth should sit just outside the bottom teeth, overlapping,

112
00:10:36,280 --> 00:10:38,960
 kind of like a lid on a box.

113
00:10:38,960 --> 00:10:45,880
 So if we're looking at the upper teeth, those upper molars are configured like this, and

114
00:10:45,880 --> 00:10:52,520
 the lower molars should be just inside so that the lingual cusps of the upper molars

115
00:10:52,520 --> 00:10:57,800
 are occluding in the central fossa of those lower molars.

116
00:10:57,800 --> 00:11:03,240
 Poster cross bite means that this is somehow messed up, and we'll look at those two situations

117
00:11:03,240 --> 00:11:04,240
 here.

118
00:11:04,240 --> 00:11:11,160
 So lingual posterior cross bite, which is the more typical example when we say posterior

119
00:11:11,160 --> 00:11:17,880
 cross bite, is when the maxillary posterior teeth are lingual to the mandibular teeth.

120
00:11:17,880 --> 00:11:20,520
 That's what we see in this example.

121
00:11:20,520 --> 00:11:26,600
 They're located too far lingual relative to where those mandibular teeth are.

122
00:11:26,600 --> 00:11:31,680
 So they still interlock, but they're in the wrong configuration.

123
00:11:31,680 --> 00:11:37,920
 The other example of posterior cross bite, which is less common, is buckle posterior

124
00:11:37,920 --> 00:11:38,920
 cross bite.

125
00:11:38,920 --> 00:11:43,440
 And that's where the maxillary posterior teeth are buckle to the mandibular teeth.

126
00:11:43,440 --> 00:11:46,160
 And that's what we see in this example.

127
00:11:46,160 --> 00:11:48,080
 So they're completely buckle.

128
00:11:48,080 --> 00:11:55,040
 This is sometimes called a brody or a scissor bite where those upper teeth sit fully outside

129
00:11:55,040 --> 00:12:06,720
 the lower teeth, and it can be really disrupt normal function with chewing specifically.

130
00:12:06,720 --> 00:12:10,280
 So let's talk a little bit about the Bolton analysis.

131
00:12:10,280 --> 00:12:18,440
 So this measures tooth size discrepancy by comparing the upper and the lower teeth.

132
00:12:18,440 --> 00:12:23,880
 So there are accepted ratios between how wide the top teeth and how wide the bottom teeth

133
00:12:23,880 --> 00:12:30,240
 should be relative to each other, in order to get them to fit together properly, again,

134
00:12:30,240 --> 00:12:36,040
 to be the upper lid that fits snugly on the lower box.

135
00:12:36,040 --> 00:12:38,720
 So we can have two mismatches here.

136
00:12:38,720 --> 00:12:45,840
 The teeth can either be too large, and that's usually on the lower arch where teeth are

137
00:12:45,840 --> 00:12:51,360
 either the incisors, maybe some premolars, somewhere that teeth are just generally a little

138
00:12:51,360 --> 00:12:58,440
 bit too large, and they may require interproximal reduction or IPR.

139
00:12:58,440 --> 00:13:03,960
 So this refers to slenderizing the teeth, just a fraction of a millimeter between the

140
00:13:03,960 --> 00:13:10,640
 proximal contacts with a strip to gain some miniscule but important amount of space.

141
00:13:10,640 --> 00:13:14,720
 Again, that's usually done on the lower incisors.

142
00:13:14,720 --> 00:13:20,200
 The teeth can also be too small, and they may require build-ups.

143
00:13:20,200 --> 00:13:24,120
 These could be in composite, veneers or crowns.

144
00:13:24,120 --> 00:13:28,080
 And the most common example of this are peg laterals.

145
00:13:28,080 --> 00:13:32,800
 They don't even have to be quite this small, but they can be just slightly smaller than

146
00:13:32,800 --> 00:13:39,080
 normal, and your bulletin analysis where you measure all the mesiodistal widths and compare

147
00:13:39,080 --> 00:13:45,880
 them with one another, could reveal that you have small upper lateral incisors, smaller

148
00:13:45,880 --> 00:13:55,520
 premolars, and you may want to consider build-ups to fit together properly.

149
00:13:55,520 --> 00:14:00,240
 So next, let's talk about the facial exam.

150
00:14:00,240 --> 00:14:03,360
 So the face is really interesting.

151
00:14:03,360 --> 00:14:10,320
 There are some proportions that we expect in a "ideal configuration."

152
00:14:10,320 --> 00:14:17,440
 So we can break the face up into vertical thirds, three roughly equal regions.

153
00:14:17,440 --> 00:14:26,000
 The upper third goes from the hairline to the glabella, which is the most prominent part

154
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:28,200
 of the forehead.

155
00:14:28,200 --> 00:14:33,440
 The middle third is from glabella to subnasality, which is the point just below the base of

156
00:14:33,440 --> 00:14:41,400
 the nose, and the lower third goes from subnasality to menton, which is the bottom of the chin.

157
00:14:41,400 --> 00:14:47,880
 Now the measurement of the upper face can often be difficult due to variability in the

158
00:14:47,880 --> 00:14:54,880
 hairline, but the good news is we can't change this dimension as orthodontist anyway, so that's

159
00:14:54,880 --> 00:14:58,920
 probably the least important of the three.

160
00:14:58,920 --> 00:15:04,480
 The most important is, of course, this lower third, asymmetrical growth of the mandible,

161
00:15:04,480 --> 00:15:08,840
 will show in the lower third, and this is where we have the most influence.

162
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:16,840
 Ideally, in this lower third, the upper lip area composes the upper third of that lower

163
00:15:16,840 --> 00:15:23,200
 third, and the lower lip and chin compose the lower two thirds of that lower facial third.

164
00:15:23,200 --> 00:15:28,560
 So we can even break that one further into smaller thirds.

165
00:15:28,560 --> 00:15:36,520
 In a horizontal dimension, we can break the face into transverse fifths, so five roughly

166
00:15:36,520 --> 00:15:40,080
 equal regions, and this is when it gets really cool.

167
00:15:40,080 --> 00:15:45,640
 So the middle fifth goes from inner campus to inner campus.

168
00:15:45,640 --> 00:15:48,320
 Campus means corner of the eye.

169
00:15:48,320 --> 00:15:56,120
 The medial two fifths go from inner campus to outer campus, so for her right and the left,

170
00:15:56,120 --> 00:16:02,520
 then the outer two fifths go from outer campus to the lateral helix of the ear, that outer

171
00:16:02,520 --> 00:16:11,400
 most portion of the ear, and then the vertical line from the inner campus point should be

172
00:16:11,400 --> 00:16:18,080
 coincident with the outer base, so that's the sides of the nose.

173
00:16:18,080 --> 00:16:23,840
 So that line should be coincident with the sides of the nose, which it is in this case,

174
00:16:23,840 --> 00:16:30,120
 and not only that, but the vertical line from the outer campus should be coincident with

175
00:16:30,120 --> 00:16:37,280
 the gonial angles of the mandible, that part of the corner of the jawline there.

176
00:16:37,280 --> 00:16:45,840
 And then the mid-pupillary distance, this is from pupil to pupil, should be the same

177
00:16:45,840 --> 00:16:54,000
 distance and perfectly correlated with the commissures or the corners of the mouth.

178
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:59,000
 So if we were to draw lines down from there, they would be coincident with the corners

179
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,480
 of the mouth.

180
00:17:01,480 --> 00:17:10,600
 So a cool way to remember these transverse fifths is going from right to left, we have

181
00:17:10,600 --> 00:17:16,520
 an ear, an eye, the nose, an eye, an ear.

182
00:17:16,520 --> 00:17:21,280
 So if you remember an ear, eye, nose, eye, ear, you can probably piece together what

183
00:17:21,280 --> 00:17:26,760
 all those fifths are in relationship to each other and to the locations of the face.

184
00:17:26,760 --> 00:17:34,280
 So pretty cool, breaking down the face into thirds and fifths.

185
00:17:34,280 --> 00:17:37,760
 So next let's talk a little bit about skeletal classification.

186
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:43,040
 So we talked about molar classification, we talked about how the incisors can be related

187
00:17:43,040 --> 00:17:44,040
 to each other.

188
00:17:44,040 --> 00:17:46,960
 Those were dental classifications.

189
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:54,480
 Now we're talking about skeletal classification and skeletal malclusions, how the jaws are

190
00:17:54,480 --> 00:17:57,080
 related to each other.

191
00:17:57,080 --> 00:18:04,520
 So in class one, skeletal occlusion, we're talking about the jaws being well related

192
00:18:04,520 --> 00:18:06,960
 to n vertical.

193
00:18:06,960 --> 00:18:12,320
 This is a vertical line dropped down from nazion, which we'll talk about later in the

194
00:18:12,320 --> 00:18:15,360
 video when we get to cephalometrics.

195
00:18:15,360 --> 00:18:22,600
 For now, this n vertical line is shown by this vertical red line out in front.

196
00:18:22,600 --> 00:18:29,080
 So whereas dental classification has to do with the anterior posterior relationship of

197
00:18:29,080 --> 00:18:33,360
 upper teeth to lower teeth, usually for smaller.

198
00:18:33,360 --> 00:18:39,720
 Clinical classification has to do with anterior relationship of the upper jaw to the lower

199
00:18:39,720 --> 00:18:41,640
 jaw.

200
00:18:41,640 --> 00:18:49,280
 So in class two, skeletal mal occlusion, we have a prognathic or pertrusive maxilla, where

201
00:18:49,280 --> 00:18:52,000
 the maxilla sticks out too far.

202
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:59,240
 Or we have a retronathic or retrusive mandible, where that is too far back from the n vertical

203
00:18:59,240 --> 00:19:03,880
 line, or we can have a combination of both.

204
00:19:03,880 --> 00:19:10,240
 I included these two terms because retronathic, retrusive, and prognathic, protrusive are

205
00:19:10,240 --> 00:19:16,280
 sometimes used interchangeably, and they're basically synonymous.

206
00:19:16,280 --> 00:19:19,640
 For class three, we have the opposite problems.

207
00:19:19,640 --> 00:19:28,240
 We have either a prognathic mandible, or a retronathic, or retrusive maxilla, or some

208
00:19:28,240 --> 00:19:31,360
 combination of both.

209
00:19:31,360 --> 00:19:39,440
 These are not exact numbers here, but class two, generally, we have about 10% of people

210
00:19:39,440 --> 00:19:42,440
 who have a pertrusive maxilla.

211
00:19:42,440 --> 00:19:47,760
 About 85% have this retronathic mandible, that's the most common problem that leaves

212
00:19:47,760 --> 00:19:51,160
 about 5% that have a combination of both.

213
00:19:51,160 --> 00:20:01,200
 And for class three, we have this is 60% for retrusive maxilla, that's the most common.

214
00:20:01,200 --> 00:20:07,480
 And then for prognathic mandible, that's about 20% of those patients, and then that leaves

215
00:20:07,480 --> 00:20:10,160
 about 20% that have a combination of both.

216
00:20:10,160 --> 00:20:14,080
 Again, those numbers are not too too important for the board exam.

217
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:20,200
 What's probably most important here is that the retrusive elements are the most common

218
00:20:20,200 --> 00:20:22,680
 for both of those malocclusions.

219
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:28,800
 All right, so we can continue to look from the profile view, this time looking more

220
00:20:28,800 --> 00:20:30,200
 at soft tissues.

221
00:20:30,200 --> 00:20:33,440
 So this is all about facial convexity.

222
00:20:33,440 --> 00:20:40,400
 We're looking at the facial plane formed by glabella, to subnase alley, to soft tissue

223
00:20:40,400 --> 00:20:49,240
 pagonion, which is the chin point, the most anterior portion of the chin in soft tissue.

224
00:20:49,240 --> 00:20:55,140
 I believe I touched on these before, but just to confirm, glabella is the area of the forehead.

225
00:20:55,140 --> 00:21:00,280
 That's typically most prominent, it's between the eyebrows, and the subnase alley is the

226
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:01,680
 base of the nose.

227
00:21:01,680 --> 00:21:08,120
 So for the facial convexity, we're basically drawing line segments from these three points

228
00:21:08,120 --> 00:21:13,080
 and seeing how straight of a line that would be.

229
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:21,040
 So for a straight profile, those line segments between glabella, base of nose, and chin point

230
00:21:21,040 --> 00:21:23,160
 are going to basically form a straight line.

231
00:21:23,160 --> 00:21:28,360
 You can see a straight line here, and the subnase alley is a little bit in front of that, but

232
00:21:28,360 --> 00:21:34,280
 that's hardly a few degrees off of that straight line.

233
00:21:34,280 --> 00:21:40,000
 So anything that's generally under 10 degrees of a slight inclination, we're going to call

234
00:21:40,000 --> 00:21:41,640
 a straight profile.

235
00:21:41,640 --> 00:21:49,480
 That's usually coincident with a class one occlusion, skeletal, and dental occlusions.

236
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:56,120
 So a convex profile is where these line segments would form a convex curve.

237
00:21:56,120 --> 00:22:00,600
 Maybe the subnase alley is way out here, and/or maybe the chin points way back there.

238
00:22:00,600 --> 00:22:04,440
 And of course, it doesn't have to be that severe, but anything that's a little bit more

239
00:22:04,440 --> 00:22:08,400
 than a 10 degree inclination, we're going to call convex.

240
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:12,160
 It's usually coincident with a class two maloclusion.

241
00:22:12,160 --> 00:22:17,000
 Then concave is where the line segments form, a concave curve.

242
00:22:17,000 --> 00:22:22,600
 So subnase alley might be back, chin point may be forward, something like that.

243
00:22:22,600 --> 00:22:29,480
 And that's usually coincident with a class three occlusion.

244
00:22:29,480 --> 00:22:34,240
 So facial divergence is rarely tested, but sometimes it does come up.

245
00:22:34,240 --> 00:22:38,460
 It's similar to facial convexity, but it is slightly different.

246
00:22:38,460 --> 00:22:42,120
 So I want to go over that discrepancy.

247
00:22:42,120 --> 00:22:50,240
 So facial divergence refers to inclination of the lower face relative to where the forehead

248
00:22:50,240 --> 00:22:52,040
 is.

249
00:22:52,040 --> 00:22:58,960
 So a straight or no facial divergence or straight divergence, I guess you could say, is very

250
00:22:58,960 --> 00:23:01,960
 similar to what we just talked about with facial convexity.

251
00:23:01,960 --> 00:23:08,200
 This is you're going to drop a vertical line down from glabella and subnase alley and chin

252
00:23:08,200 --> 00:23:11,880
 point are very close to that line.

253
00:23:11,880 --> 00:23:19,560
 Posterior divergent means that the entire line dropped down from the forehead to the

254
00:23:19,560 --> 00:23:21,640
 chin goes posteriorly.

255
00:23:21,640 --> 00:23:29,080
 So subnase alley is a little bit more posterior and chin points even more posterior.

256
00:23:29,080 --> 00:23:33,280
 Interior divergent means we drop that same line down, but this time subnase alley is

257
00:23:33,280 --> 00:23:37,640
 a little bit more forward and chin points even more forward.

258
00:23:37,640 --> 00:23:40,440
 So it's not quite the same as convexity.

259
00:23:40,440 --> 00:23:46,560
 This is talking about everything is kind of moving posteriorly when we go up to down.

260
00:23:46,560 --> 00:23:51,520
 Everything is moving anteriorly as we go from up to down.

261
00:23:51,520 --> 00:23:57,120
 Now the nice part about this is that this does usually fit with a class two maloclusion,

262
00:23:57,120 --> 00:24:07,880
 as you could see, and this would usually coincide with a class three maloclusion.

263
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:13,560
 Now we can zoom in a little bit more on profile and talk about specific angles that are formed

264
00:24:13,560 --> 00:24:16,040
 in the soft tissue.

265
00:24:16,040 --> 00:24:22,360
 So the nasolabial angle is formed between the columnella of the nose, that's this bottom

266
00:24:22,360 --> 00:24:29,840
 part of the nose here that separates the two nostrils and the philtrum.

267
00:24:29,840 --> 00:24:33,480
 That's that vertical indentation of the upper lip.

268
00:24:33,480 --> 00:24:35,240
 And that makes sense.

269
00:24:35,240 --> 00:24:40,160
 This is nasophore nose and labial for lip.

270
00:24:40,160 --> 00:24:44,720
 So this angle should be approximately 90 degrees.

271
00:24:44,720 --> 00:24:51,000
 It's determined by a bunch of different things, AP position of the maxilla, AP position of

272
00:24:51,000 --> 00:24:58,040
 the upper incisors, and particularly vertical position of the nasal tip, like if the nose

273
00:24:58,040 --> 00:25:05,680
 is upturned in a younger child, that can make this nasolabial angle a lot larger.

274
00:25:05,680 --> 00:25:10,480
 But there's quite a bit of range that is perfectly acceptable here.

275
00:25:10,480 --> 00:25:17,160
 The thickness of the upper lip could also contribute to nasolabial angle.

276
00:25:17,160 --> 00:25:24,920
 Going down, mentolabial angle is between the lower lip and the chin.

277
00:25:24,920 --> 00:25:28,080
 So mento for chin, labial for lip.

278
00:25:28,080 --> 00:25:31,880
 This should be around 120 degrees.

279
00:25:31,880 --> 00:25:40,000
 This one's affected by lower incisor position and vertical height of the lower facial third.

280
00:25:40,000 --> 00:25:46,760
 It's sometimes also called a mentolabial fold or mentolabial sulcus.

281
00:25:46,760 --> 00:25:50,880
 And lastly we have the cervicomental angle.

282
00:25:50,880 --> 00:25:59,560
 This one goes from menton, which is base of the chin again, to our point, or reflex point,

283
00:25:59,560 --> 00:26:03,800
 as a depth of the throat, and then to the base of the neck.

284
00:26:03,800 --> 00:26:07,320
 This one again, well this one has a pretty large range.

285
00:26:07,320 --> 00:26:12,720
 It goes from 90 degrees to up to 120 degrees.

286
00:26:12,720 --> 00:26:23,440
 Other considerations of this one are gender, soft tissue sag from aging and body weight.

287
00:26:23,440 --> 00:26:30,840
 Alright so lips are arguably, if we're talking about the soft tissue paradigm, the most important

288
00:26:30,840 --> 00:26:38,800
 soft tissue consideration of a modern orthodontist, outside of the gingiva and the peridontal tissues.

289
00:26:38,800 --> 00:26:47,600
 So how I remember the lips are the three P's, that's position, posture and proportions.

290
00:26:47,600 --> 00:26:56,880
 So for lip position, we draw a line that's tangent to the nose and the chin.

291
00:26:56,880 --> 00:27:04,280
 So this line right here, tangent to both of those two points, is called rickett line,

292
00:27:04,280 --> 00:27:06,600
 or aesthetic line.

293
00:27:06,600 --> 00:27:13,520
 So the lower lip should be on this line and the upper lip should be slightly behind the

294
00:27:13,520 --> 00:27:15,200
 line.

295
00:27:15,200 --> 00:27:23,960
 If both of your lips are ahead of the line, they're considered prominent or protrusive.

296
00:27:23,960 --> 00:27:28,920
 If they're behind the line, they're considered retrusive.

297
00:27:28,920 --> 00:27:34,080
 So that's a quick and dirty way of judging AP position of the lips.

298
00:27:34,080 --> 00:27:41,140
 Note that this is not very useful if the nose and/or chin are excessively large.

299
00:27:41,140 --> 00:27:47,560
 This is most useful for patients in their adolescent ears.

300
00:27:47,560 --> 00:27:57,080
 So for lip posture, our second P, we're looking for lip competence at rest.

301
00:27:57,080 --> 00:28:04,080
 So we have three to four millimeters of separation at rest, which we see here, and mantelis strain

302
00:28:04,080 --> 00:28:06,600
 on closure.

303
00:28:06,600 --> 00:28:11,920
 So this means that in order for them to get a lip seal, they would have to strain their

304
00:28:11,920 --> 00:28:18,080
 mantelis muscle, so we can see all erased the drawing, so you can appreciate that amount

305
00:28:18,080 --> 00:28:22,800
 of muscle strain of the mantelis.

306
00:28:22,800 --> 00:28:29,160
 So the muscles have to tighten up to get the lips to seal over the teeth.

307
00:28:29,160 --> 00:28:34,880
 So the combination of separation at rest and having to strain that mantelis to get the

308
00:28:34,880 --> 00:28:38,200
 lips to close is lip incompetence.

309
00:28:38,200 --> 00:28:43,920
 Lip competence means that the lips can form a seal over the teeth at rest without any

310
00:28:43,920 --> 00:28:46,760
 amount of strain.

311
00:28:46,760 --> 00:28:51,880
 For lip proportions, we're simply looking at their dimension.

312
00:28:51,880 --> 00:28:58,080
 Are they thick and full, or are they thin and flat?

313
00:28:58,080 --> 00:29:03,800
 How much vermilion, the actual lip tissue, do they display?

314
00:29:03,800 --> 00:29:08,800
 So this one considers kind of all three planes of space.

315
00:29:08,800 --> 00:29:13,960
 There's an AP component, a vertical component, and probably even a transverse component.

316
00:29:13,960 --> 00:29:18,440
 For posture, this is pretty much a vertical issue.

317
00:29:18,440 --> 00:29:23,520
 For position, it's pretty much an AP issue.

318
00:29:23,520 --> 00:29:29,160
 So those are the three P's of the lips.

319
00:29:29,160 --> 00:29:38,040
 Incisor display refers to how much of the incisors show, either at rest or on smile.

320
00:29:38,040 --> 00:29:43,920
 So incisor display at rest, we're looking for about two to four millimeters being aesthetically

321
00:29:43,920 --> 00:29:46,160
 pleasing.

322
00:29:46,160 --> 00:29:50,160
 So this is a little bit of the incisor showing.

323
00:29:50,160 --> 00:29:56,160
 Again, if you're at rest, maybe your lips are touching, you have lip seal, that's acceptable

324
00:29:56,160 --> 00:29:57,840
 as well.

325
00:29:57,840 --> 00:30:04,720
 On smile, we want all of the upper incisors to show with about one to two millimeters

326
00:30:04,720 --> 00:30:07,760
 of gingiva above them.

327
00:30:07,760 --> 00:30:14,720
 So that's not including the papilla area, we want some little bit amount of gingiva showing

328
00:30:14,720 --> 00:30:20,640
 above those teeth, that's a youthful look.

329
00:30:20,640 --> 00:30:27,160
 Any more than two millimeters is considered a gummy smile by the lay person.

330
00:30:27,160 --> 00:30:36,800
 About 75% of the incisors showing on smile is considered the minimum acceptable amount.

331
00:30:36,800 --> 00:30:38,480
 And finally, buckle quarters.

332
00:30:38,480 --> 00:30:45,320
 So this refers to the dark space between the maxillary posterior teeth and the corner of

333
00:30:45,320 --> 00:30:46,920
 the mouth upon smile.

334
00:30:46,920 --> 00:30:52,680
 So that's this amount of negative space.

335
00:30:52,680 --> 00:30:59,160
 Sometimes it's referred to if you have a collapsed narrow arch form, it can result in a lot of

336
00:30:59,160 --> 00:31:07,080
 shadowing negative space referred to as wide buckle quarters.

337
00:31:07,080 --> 00:31:12,480
 Having treatment that involves lateral expansion of the premolars and molars into this space

338
00:31:12,480 --> 00:31:19,480
 can improve the transverse smile dimension and create a more medium or even narrow buckle

339
00:31:19,480 --> 00:31:25,320
 quarters if there is minimal dark space in those corners of the mouth.

340
00:31:25,320 --> 00:31:33,240
 So a broad arch will result in narrow buckle quarters while a narrow arch will result in

341
00:31:33,240 --> 00:31:39,560
 wide buckle quarters.

342
00:31:39,560 --> 00:31:46,880
 And the last part we'll talk about are cephalometrics, which look at both the teeth and the face

343
00:31:46,880 --> 00:31:50,640
 in a very unique perspective.

344
00:31:50,640 --> 00:31:57,040
 So the lateral cephalometric gives us a lot of information about a patient's heart tissue

345
00:31:57,040 --> 00:32:02,760
 as well as soft tissue structures from the profile view.

346
00:32:02,760 --> 00:32:10,280
 So I've marked a bunch of cephalometric landmarks here that we can go over together.

347
00:32:10,280 --> 00:32:13,520
 So our first one we'll talk about is this one back here.

348
00:32:13,520 --> 00:32:22,520
 It's not very commonly used but it's called the Bolton Point and that's BO for short.

349
00:32:22,520 --> 00:32:30,080
 It's the highest point in the upward curvature of the occipital bone around the center of

350
00:32:30,080 --> 00:32:40,640
 this gigantic frame and magnum where the spinal cord runs from the brain down here.

351
00:32:40,640 --> 00:32:47,640
 Next we have the Bayesion, which is this point right here, that's BA for short.

352
00:32:47,640 --> 00:32:54,600
 And the Bayesion is the lowest point of the anterior margin of the foramen magnum and it

353
00:32:54,600 --> 00:33:05,120
 points towards the dens or the odontoid process which is right here of c2, that's the axis

354
00:33:05,120 --> 00:33:06,120
 of the vertebrae.

355
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:10,080
 We'll talk a little bit more about vertebrae in a little bit.

356
00:33:10,080 --> 00:33:18,400
 The next point is the Articulari ar for short and that's right here, that's the intersection

357
00:33:18,400 --> 00:33:25,040
 between the zygomatic arch which is running here and the posterior border of the ramus.

358
00:33:25,040 --> 00:33:30,580
 Note that there's actually, you can see both Ramai, you can see one here and then one further

359
00:33:30,580 --> 00:33:37,520
 back here and this means that the patient was either there's some degree of asymmetry

360
00:33:37,520 --> 00:33:42,480
 between the right and left sides or probably more possible.

361
00:33:42,480 --> 00:33:48,400
 The patient wasn't positioned properly in the imaging unit.

362
00:33:48,400 --> 00:33:53,720
 So in that case we just split the difference between the left and right sides and we pick

363
00:33:53,720 --> 00:33:57,440
 in the middle of those two projections.

364
00:33:57,440 --> 00:34:04,200
 Next point is the Purion which is up here, PO for short and that's the highest point

365
00:34:04,200 --> 00:34:13,520
 of the external auditory meatus, that's the outer ear opening and this part is the ear

366
00:34:13,520 --> 00:34:18,240
 rod of the cephalometric machine.

367
00:34:18,240 --> 00:34:27,560
 So we would imagine that's pretty close to Purion which is the outer ear hole in the skull.

368
00:34:27,560 --> 00:34:36,840
 So next let's go to this one, that's Condillion CO and it's the most posterior and superior

369
00:34:36,840 --> 00:34:40,840
 point on the outline of the condler head.

370
00:34:40,840 --> 00:34:47,360
 The condyle can be pretty tough to see in a cephalometric.

371
00:34:47,360 --> 00:34:55,520
 Next we'll go over here, this is the Terego maxillary fissure PTM and that's the base

372
00:34:55,520 --> 00:35:02,920
 of this fissure, it's often a teardrop shape that runs along the back of the maxilla.

373
00:35:02,920 --> 00:35:09,000
 A bunch of vessels and nerves run in and out of that fissure.

374
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:16,480
 Next we'll go up here, this is Cella S for short, it's the midpoint of the Cella Terseca

375
00:35:16,480 --> 00:35:22,640
 which is the saddle in which the pituitary gland lives.

376
00:35:22,640 --> 00:35:30,560
 This point over here is Orbitale, that's the lowest point on the inferior margin of the

377
00:35:30,560 --> 00:35:34,000
 orbit where the eye lives.

378
00:35:34,000 --> 00:35:44,240
 Up here is Nazion or N and that's the anterior point on the intersection between the nasal

379
00:35:44,240 --> 00:35:50,280
 bone down here and the frontal bone up here.

380
00:35:50,280 --> 00:35:56,800
 You can almost appreciate the little suture running here between those two bones which

381
00:35:56,800 --> 00:36:00,200
 is pretty cool.

382
00:36:00,200 --> 00:36:10,880
 Alright next let's go here, this point is the anterior nasal spine and that's the sharp

383
00:36:10,880 --> 00:36:17,200
 projection of the maxilla anteriorly and then we have the posterior nasal spine which is

384
00:36:17,200 --> 00:36:27,320
 usually right below the PTM and that's the sharp projection of the palatine bones posteriorly.

385
00:36:27,320 --> 00:36:31,840
 That's where that hard palate ends.

386
00:36:31,840 --> 00:36:41,880
 Right here we have A point and that's the innermost portion, innermost point on the contour of

387
00:36:41,880 --> 00:36:48,360
 the maxilla, that's the innermost point on the contour of the maxillary bone.

388
00:36:48,360 --> 00:36:54,440
 Likewise we have the B point which again is the innermost point on the contour of the

389
00:36:54,440 --> 00:36:58,360
 mandibular bone.

390
00:36:58,360 --> 00:37:06,280
 Down here we have three right up next to each other, this one is Pagonion, that's the most

391
00:37:06,280 --> 00:37:11,320
 anterior point of the chin, you may remember we had a soft tissue Pagonion which is the

392
00:37:11,320 --> 00:37:15,480
 soft tissue counterpoint to this hard tissue landmark.

393
00:37:15,480 --> 00:37:24,220
 Down here we have menton which is ME for short, that's the most inferior point on the chin,

394
00:37:24,220 --> 00:37:28,080
 again we have the soft tissue menton which we talked about before when we were talking

395
00:37:28,080 --> 00:37:36,000
 about facial thirds and other things and the point between Pagonion and menton is called

396
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:41,840
 Nafion and GN are the letters there.

397
00:37:41,840 --> 00:37:49,360
 Gonian is back here, that's G-O, that's the midpoint on the contour connecting the Ramis

398
00:37:49,360 --> 00:37:57,000
 and the body of the mandible and I believe that's all the points we have here.

399
00:37:57,000 --> 00:38:05,640
 Other structures to point out we have the frontal sinus up here, the ethmoid air cells,

400
00:38:05,640 --> 00:38:12,440
 we have the maxillary sinus this gigantic thing here, the sphenoid sinus, the mastoid

401
00:38:12,440 --> 00:38:24,840
 air cells, here is C1 which is the atlas, C2 with the dens running up here, that's the

402
00:38:24,840 --> 00:38:34,440
 axis, we have C3, C4 and we can keep going down, this is the hyoid bone right in here,

403
00:38:34,440 --> 00:38:41,880
 the epiglottis here and of course we can appreciate the soft tissue profile with the nose, the

404
00:38:41,880 --> 00:38:49,040
 lips and the chin, so lots of stuff to get from a cephalometric x-ray and some really

405
00:38:49,040 --> 00:38:57,200
 cool stuff there, here's a summary of everything that we just talked about, so from those points

406
00:38:57,200 --> 00:39:03,160
 we can gather some important information like reference planes that connect two of those

407
00:39:03,160 --> 00:39:10,240
 points together, for example we could connect cella and nazion and get the SN plane which

408
00:39:10,240 --> 00:39:18,400
 represents the anterior cranial base, if we connect poorion and orbitally we get what's

409
00:39:18,400 --> 00:39:24,960
 called the frankfort horizontal plane, which is an incredibly important reference plane,

410
00:39:24,960 --> 00:39:32,200
 if we connect ANS and PNS we get the palatal plane, if we connect lower six which is the

411
00:39:32,200 --> 00:39:39,360
 cusp tip of the lower first molar and lower one, the incisal edge of the lower incisor,

412
00:39:39,360 --> 00:39:44,400
 we get a occlusal plane and if we connect gonion and nafion we get the mandibular plane

413
00:39:44,400 --> 00:39:50,040
 which is another really, really important reference plane, the sussuni analysis states

414
00:39:50,040 --> 00:39:57,920
 that all five of these reference planes should intersect at around the back of the head,

415
00:39:57,920 --> 00:40:04,160
 the occiput of the head, if they intersect earlier the patient is considered hyperdivergent,

416
00:40:04,160 --> 00:40:10,220
 if they intersect later they're considered hypo-divergent, just one example of how these

417
00:40:10,220 --> 00:40:18,760
 reference planes can be used diagnostically, so probably the most important use of the

418
00:40:18,760 --> 00:40:24,920
 cephalometric is a cephalometric analysis, this is used to evaluate the relationship

419
00:40:24,920 --> 00:40:32,360
 of the jaws and the dental units to each other, this used to be done on acetate paper, but

420
00:40:32,360 --> 00:40:38,600
 today we use computer software and mark all of those landmarks we just went over and

421
00:40:38,600 --> 00:40:45,400
 in the software would develop a tracing from which you can gather all these linear and

422
00:40:45,400 --> 00:40:52,520
 angular measurements, these measurements allow you to evaluate the relationship of the jaws

423
00:40:52,520 --> 00:40:58,240
 and the teeth to each other and the cranial base, to which everything is attached, and

424
00:40:58,240 --> 00:41:07,080
 you would compare your patient to numerical norms for their age, racial, and ethnic groups,

425
00:41:07,080 --> 00:41:16,920
 so sna is a very common angular measurement, this is the angle from cella to nazion to

426
00:41:16,920 --> 00:41:24,040
 a point, and it represents the relationship of the maxilla to the cranial base, a bigger

427
00:41:24,040 --> 00:41:32,760
 angle means that the maxilla is further forward, a smaller angle means that the maxilla is

428
00:41:32,760 --> 00:41:42,760
 further back, snb, cella to nazion to b point refers to the relationship of the mandible

429
00:41:42,760 --> 00:41:48,440
 to the cranial base, a bigger angle means the mandibles more forward, smaller angle means

430
00:41:48,440 --> 00:41:54,920
 the mandibles further back, and then a and b kind of combines those two together, and

431
00:41:54,920 --> 00:42:03,000
 this instead looks at the relationship of the maxilla to the mandible, and this is the one

432
00:42:03,000 --> 00:42:11,520
 that comes up on the board exam quite frequently, so we go from a point to nazion to b point,

433
00:42:11,520 --> 00:42:20,680
 and this angle is very, very small, if we're at around zero degrees, or below, we're in

434
00:42:20,680 --> 00:42:26,680
 the negatives, that is considered a class three skeletal malocclusion, is again, we're talking

435
00:42:26,680 --> 00:42:34,080
 about the jaw units, we're not looking specifically at what the teeth are doing, so zero or below

436
00:42:34,080 --> 00:42:41,680
